# Windsurf Agent Ruleset

## Coding Pattern Preferences

Always prefer simple solutions.
Avoid duplication of code wherever possible, which means checking for other areas of the codebase that might already have similar code and functionality.
Avoid premature abstraction. Only refactor or abstract code into shared utilities, base classes, or containers when a repeatable pattern has emerged (2 or more clear instances). Prioritize clarity and simplicity over theoretical reuse.
Write code that takes into account the different environments: dev, test, and prod.
You are careful to only make changes that are requested or you are confident are well understood and related to the change being requested.
When fixing an issue or bug, do not introduce a new pattern or technology without first exhausting all options for the existing implementation. If you finally do this, make sure to remove the old implementation afterwards so we don't have duplicate logic.
Keep the codebase very clean and organized.
Avoid writing scripts in files if possible, especially if the script is likely only to be run once.
Avoid having files over 200-300 lines of code. Refactor at that point.
While the standard guideline is to keep files under 200-300 lines of code, specific exceptions apply for complex UI components:

- Complex UI Components: Components with sophisticated visualization, interaction patterns, or extensive state management (such as Calendar/Gantt charts, data visualizations, and multi-stage forms) may exceed this limit when justified by their cohesion and functionality
- Implementation Requirements:
- Document specific justification in component headers, citing the relevant Design Guideline section (e.g., Section 4.6 for Calendar components, Section 4.3 for visualization components)
- Request user confirmation before implementation when exceeding the line limit
- Consider logical subcomponent extraction while preserving the parent component's API cohesion
- Split by logical responsibility when possible, without fragmenting related functionality
- For utility functions and non-UI code, strictly adhere to the 200-line maximum with no exceptions

Mocking data is only needed for tests, never mock data for dev or prod.
Never add stubbing or fake data patterns to code that affects the dev or prod environments.
Never overwrite my .env file without first asking and confirming.
When making performance improvements, always measure impact with benchmarks before and after.
Prefer asyncio for I/O bound operations, threads for CPU-bound operations.

## Technical Requirements

Clearly define and document your selected database system.
Clearly define and document your selected backend API architecture.
Clearly define and document your frontend framework and its architecture.
Use OpenAPI (Swagger) to document all FastAPI endpoints.
Use Alembic for all database migrations (Postgres).
Document the selected frontend state management library (Redux Toolkit or Zustand).

## Documentation and Comments

Write concise, meaningful comments and documentation only when they clarify functionality or complex logic.
Avoid personal pronouns ("I", "we", etc.) in comments and documentation.
Document all functions, classes, and modules clearly, stating purpose, behavior, inputs, outputs, assumptions, and side effects.
Maintain up-to-date docstrings, clearly noting special considerations or edge cases.
Automate generation of windsurfmemory.md using Docusaurus or equivalent.

## Error Handling

Use specific and purposeful exception handling; avoid broad try-except blocks unless catching and handling specific exceptions explicitly.
Anticipate common errors and proactively handle or mitigate them in your code.
Where possible, provide actionable error messages to assist debugging.

## Logging

Use an established logging framework consistently throughout the project for traceability and monitoring.
Log informative, warning, and error-level events clearly to facilitate debugging.
Automate logging level control via .env: DEBUG for dev, INFO for prod.

## Dependencies & Configuration

Clearly document all project dependencies within a dedicated file (e.g., dependencies.py, requirements.txt, or equivalent).
Pass dependencies explicitly through class and function constructors when appropriate.
Maintain all configuration settings within a centralized, clearly structured configuration file (e.g., config.py).
Never hardcode credentials, API keys, or secrets in the codebase.
Always explicitly request credentials from the user when:

- Setting up a new integration that requires API keys or tokens
- Connecting to cloud services (AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, etc.)
- Accessing third-party systems requiring authentication
- Deploying to environments that require service credentials
  Wait for explicit provision of credentials before proceeding with implementation.
  Use environment variables or secure credential storage for all sensitive information.

## Naming Conventions

Clearly distinguish private class variables using leading underscores (e.g., \_private_var).
Use consistent and descriptive naming for functions, variables, and classes.
Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constant values.

## Execution Flow & Tests

Adopt a test-driven development (TDD) approach: Write tests before implementation.
Always run existing tests to ensure new code integrates smoothly.
Update tests continuously to reflect the current state of your project.
Keep tests focused, clean, and up-to-date.
Ensure all features have minimum unit, integration, and if applicable, end-to-end tests.
Aim for 80%+ test coverage as a baseline.

## Version Control & Quality Gates

Commit frequently after each unit of functionality passes validation.
Never commit untested or unvalidated code to the repository.
Require explicit human QA approval before committing any functional changes.
Write descriptive commit messages following conventional commit format (feat:, fix:, refactor:, etc.).
Create release tags for all deployable milestones with semantic versioning.
Push commits immediately after local validation to maintain team synchronization.
Document the validation process used in commit messages when relevant.
Use meaningful branch names that reflect the feature or fix being implemented.

## Clean & Maintainable Code

Write readable and maintainable code, focusing on simplicity and clarity.
Ensure functions are concise, focused, and single-purpose.
Follow the DRY principle (Don't Repeat Yourself).
When refactoring, clearly document the reason and context in the docstring to prevent regressions.
Clearly document and comment any temporary fixes or assumptions made during debugging.
Keep variables and function names descriptive.

## Development Workflow

Focus on the areas of code relevant to the task.
Do not touch code that is unrelated to the task.
Write thorough tests for all major functionality.
Avoid making major changes to the patterns and architecture of how a feature works, after it has shown to work well, unless explicitly instructed.
Always write the tests first.
Always run the tests to make sure the code works.
When given a task, write code and run it until it works well.
Break down tasks into clear, manageable sub-components.
Plan thoroughly before implementing new code.
Consider integration points and interactions with existing components.
Anticipate potential errors, edge cases, and how they’ll be handled.
Keep frontend, backend, user interaction, and developer experience considerations in mind.
Implement, test, validate, then refactor to maintain code quality and integrity.

## File Structure

Clearly document and maintain a standardized file and directory structure.
Keep initialization files (e.g., init.py) empty.
While maintaining a maximum of 3 levels of nesting for directories as a general rule, complex UI components may require deeper component hierarchies in their internal structure. Document component hierarchies for complex UI elements to ensure maintainability.

## Code Style

Adhere to a consistent coding style guide throughout the project.
Standardize string delimiters (always use single quotes consistently throughout the codebase).
Enforce linting via pre-commit hooks.

## Rewrite, Improve, and Refactor

When refactoring or fixing code, make sure to keep the code clean and easy to understand without duplicating code.
Keep the code clean and easy to understand.
Keep the code DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself).
After fixing an issue, mention the case in the docstring so future changes won't break it again.

## Tests

Always write the tests first.
Always run the tests to make sure the code works.
Always keep the tests clean and up to date.
Always run the tests in the venv.

## Debugging & Issue Resolution

When encountering unclear issues or edge cases, leverage detailed logging statements or debug prints temporarily for insights.
Immediately remove temporary debugging code once the issue is resolved.
Always document the issue and resolution clearly in comments or docstrings to assist future developers.

## Async Programming

Use asynchronous functions (async) whenever concurrency or non-blocking operations enhance performance or responsiveness.
Clearly document the rationale for synchronous vs. asynchronous implementations.
Prefer asyncio for I/O bound operations, threads for CPU-bound operations.

## Memory and Knowledge Management

Maintain a dedicated project knowledge base or memory file (windsurfmemory.md) to document specifications, project-specific conventions, APIs, development instructions, and insights gained.
Regularly update and consult this memory file throughout the development lifecycle.
Automate generation and update of windsurfmemory.md for live project context updates.

## Implementation Planning & Progress Tracking

Treat the ImplementationPlan.md in the docs folder as the authoritative source of truth for product development.
Generate BuildChecklist.md at project initialization containing all tasks extracted from the implementation plan.
Update BuildChecklist.md continuously with task completion status, timestamps, and detailed notes.
Document implementation deviations, errors encountered, and resolution strategies for each completed task.
Maintain task granularity that aligns with Git commit units for traceability.
Reference BuildChecklist.md before starting any new development work to ensure alignment.
Include relevant file paths, function names, and architectural decisions in task completion notes.
Cross-reference completed tasks with corresponding Git commits for full audit trail.

## Proactive Planning & Future-Proofing

Always anticipate future scaling, performance, and extensibility when designing your solution.
Clearly document planned future extensions or potential limitations within your codebase.
Always plan the code before writing it.
Think about how the new code will fit into the existing codebase.
Think about how the new code will interact with the other parts of the codebase.
Think about how the new code will handle errors and edge cases.
Think about how the new code will be used by the frontend.
Think about how the new code will be used by the users.
Think about how the new code will be used by the developers.
Cross-Cutting Architectural Concerns:

1. For features like internationalization, accessibility, and responsive design:

- Implement the core infrastructure as part of the initial architecture
- Add specific implementations (languages, accommodations) as explicitly tasked
- Follow the "progressive enhancement" pattern
- basic functionality first, enhancements later
- Never remove or refactor core infrastructure even if specific implementations are delayed

2. When implementing specific components:

- Reference the Design Guidelines to ensure alignment with long-term architecture
- Include cross-cutting support at creation time (i18n readiness, accessibility attributes)
- Document any deferred implementations that will need future tasks

## Locality of Behaviour

Prioritize locality of behaviour for feature modules.
Avoid premature fragmentation — module splits require minimum complexity/use-case threshold.
Maintain global registries / index files for agent reference.
Auto-generate module READMEs or internal docs to reduce future flow actions.
Refactor shared logic responsibly: only after patterns emerge (≥2 duplications).
Maintain healthy folder depth (max 3 levels by default).
Optimize folder/module granularity to align with CI/CD and build performance.
Use self-auditing prompts to regularly check locality health.
Integration Implementation Strategy:

1. All integrations must follow a well-defined pattern:

- Use adapter pattern to isolate third-party code
- Implement interface contracts with strict typing
- Create dedicated configuration sections for each integration

2. Integration development workflow:

- Begin with specification of data exchange contract
- Implement minimum viable integration first
- Extend capabilities only when specifically tasked
- Document all integration points in central registry 3. Default approach for third-party systems:
- Manual import/export initially to validate requirements
- API integration only after manual process validation
- Automated sync only for frequently used data

## Credit Consumption Rules

Batch operations to group related changes in a single flow.
Minimize "search thrashing" by maintaining and referring to a pre-generated module map.
Deduplicate actions: always check for existing utilities before creating new ones.
Defer non-critical formatting/cleanup to low-priority batch maintenance cycles.
Generate pre-change impact maps for global refactors.
Self-throttle if credit consumption exceeds task-specific thresholds.
Log credit consumption per task batch to improve future estimations.

## Maintainability Rules

Apply the Single Responsibility Principle across modules, classes, and functions.
Use meaningful, consistent, human-friendly naming.
Auto-generate or maintain internal documentation (README / docstring) per module.
Follow established project conventions for structure, imports, comments, and style.
Avoid tight coupling between modules unless justified.
Refactor shared logic into utilities only after 2+ duplications.
Create onboarding cheatsheets (optional but recommended).
Enforce pre-commit hooks for formatting, linting, and hygiene checks.
Prefer modularization, but avoid excessive micro-modules.
Batch dependency updates and include impact summaries.
Track codebase health via scorecards (optional).
Implement self-healing practices: propose fixes and improvements proactively.
Annotate temporary fixes with timestamps and clear intent for future cleanup.
